@page "/batching"

<PageTitle>Batching - Linq2GraphQL</PageTitle>

<h1 class="page-heading">Batching</h1>
<p class="lead text-secondary">
    A page often needs several unrelated queries. A batch runs them together: you add each query with a callback
    for its result, and the batch merges them into as few requests as possible, sends those in parallel and hands
    every result to its callback.
</p>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="first-batch">Run queries together</h2>
    <p>
        Start a batch with <code>CreateBatch()</code> on the generated client, add queries with <code>Query</code>
        and finish each with <code>OnResult</code> instead of <code>ExecuteAsync</code>. One
        <code>ExecuteAsync</code> on the batch runs them all:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@FirstBatchCSharp"/>
    <CodeBlock Title="Output" Language="text" Code="@FirstBatchOutput"/>
    <p>
        The three queries travel as one GraphQL operation. Each root field gets an alias, <code>b0</code>,
        <code>b1</code> and so on, and its variables the alias as a prefix, so queries for the same field with
        different arguments can't collide:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Sent (formatted)" Language="text" Code="@FirstBatchQuery"/>
    <ul>
        <li>
            The lambda passed to <code>Query</code> receives the client and runs when the batch executes, not
            when you add it. It may return <code>null</code> to add nothing.
        </li>
        <li>
            Callbacks run after every response has arrived, one at a time, in the order the queries were added.
            Assigning to local variables, as above, is safe. <code>OnResultAsync</code> takes an async callback
            and awaits it before the next one.
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>OnResult</code> wraps the query in a batch item without changing the query, so the same query can
            still be executed on its own or added again. Each item belongs to one batch, and a batch executes once.
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>CreateBatch</code> works on a generated client class. Through a client interface, such as one
            injected as <code>IStarWarsClient</code>, use <code>GraphBatch.Create(client)</code> instead.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="many">Many queries</h2>
    <p>
        Batches are built in code, so a loop can add one query per item. <code>MaxItemsPerRequest</code> caps how
        many queries go into one request; the rest go into further requests, which are sent in parallel:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@LoopCSharp"/>
    <CodeBlock Title="Output" Language="text" Code="@LoopOutput"/>
    <p>
        Six films with a cap of three make two requests. Their responses can arrive in any order, but the
        callbacks still run in the order the films were added.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="conditional">Conditional queries</h2>
    <p>
        <code>QueryIf</code> adds a query only when its condition holds, so the chain doesn't need <code>if</code>
        blocks around it. The condition is a <code>bool</code>, or a <code>Func&lt;bool&gt;</code> that is
        evaluated when the batch executes:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@ConditionalCSharp"/>
    <CodeBlock Title="Output" Language="text" Code="@ConditionalOutput"/>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="mutations">Mutations and several clients</h2>
    <p>
        Queries and mutations can't share an operation, so a batch that has both sends them separately. Mutations
        are merged too, but their requests are sent one after another, and the server runs the fields of one
        mutation in order, so a batch of mutations runs in the order you added them.
    </p>
    <p>
        Each query is sent with the client it was built from. A query built from a client derived with
        <code>WithAccessToken</code> or <code>WithRequestConfiguration</code> goes into a request of its own, with
        that client's headers.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="errors">Errors</h2>
    <p>
        Like a single query, <code>ExecuteAsync</code> throws when something failed, and
        <code>ExecuteWithResultAsync</code> returns the errors instead. Either way, a failure in one query doesn't
        cost the others their results:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@ResultCSharp"/>
    <CodeBlock Title="Output" Language="text" Code="@ResultOutput"/>
    <p>
        Each error's path starts with its query's alias, so <code>b1</code> above is the second query added; queries
        that <code>QueryIf</code> skipped still count. With <code>ExecuteAsync</code> the callbacks run first and the
        exception comes after them:
    </p>
    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table table-vcenter card-table border">
            <thead>
            <tr><th>Failure</th><th>Callback</th><th>ExecuteAsync throws</th><th>ExecuteWithResultAsync</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>A field returns an error</td>
                <td>Runs, with the data that came back (often <code>null</code>)</td>
                <td><code>GraphQueryExecutionException</code></td>
                <td><code>Errors</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>
                    The server returns <code>"data": null</code>, because a non-null root field failed, or rejects
                    the request before running it
                </td>
                <td>Doesn't run for any query in that request</td>
                <td><code>GraphQueryExecutionException</code></td>
                <td><code>Errors</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The HTTP request fails or times out</td>
                <td>Doesn't run for any query in that request</td>
                <td><code>GraphQueryRequestException</code> or <code>TaskCanceledException</code></td>
                <td><code>Failures</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The batch is cancelled</td>
                <td>No callback runs</td>
                <td><code>OperationCanceledException</code></td>
                <td>Throws as well</td>
            </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        In <code>Parallel</code> mode every query is its own request, so a query whose non-null root field fails
        only loses its own result, and its callback runs with <code>null</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        When several requests of a batch fail, <code>ExecuteAsync</code> throws an <code>AggregateException</code>
        holding one exception per request. <code>GraphBatchResult.EnsureNoErrors()</code> throws the same way.
        See <a href="error-handling">error handling</a> for the exceptions themselves.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="limits">Server limits</h2>
    <p>
        Servers limit how large or how costly a request may be, and a merged request grows with every query in it.
        GraphQL has no way to ask a server for its limits, so the batch finds out: when the server rejects a request
        before running it (an HTTP 413, or errors without a path and no data, which is how size and cost limits
        answer), the batch sends its queries again in two halves, down to single queries if it has to. Nothing ran,
        so resending is safe, for mutations too.
    </p>
    <p>
        When every query got through once the requests were small enough, the client remembers the largest size
        that was accepted, and later batches on the same client start there. A query that is rejected even on its
        own fails with the server's error, and then nothing is remembered: the rejection was about that query, not
        about size. Set <code>SplitOnRejection</code> to <code>false</code> to report rejections without splitting.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="options">Options</h2>
    <p>Pass a callback to <code>CreateBatch</code> to change them:</p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@OptionsCSharp"/>
    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table table-vcenter card-table border">
            <thead>
            <tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><code>Mode</code></td>
                <td><code>SingleRequest</code></td>
                <td>
                    <code>SingleRequest</code> merges queries into as few requests as possible.
                    <code>Parallel</code> sends every query as its own request, so a failure, or a slow field,
                    affects only its own query.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>MaxItemsPerRequest</code></td>
                <td>100</td>
                <td>The most queries merged into one request.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>MaxDegreeOfParallelism</code></td>
                <td>4</td>
                <td>The most requests in flight at once. Mutations are always sent one at a time.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>SplitOnRejection</code></td>
                <td><code>true</code></td>
                <td>Split a request the server rejects into halves and send them again.</td>
            </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    private const string FirstBatchCSharp = """
        string title = null;
        string name = null;
        List<string> planets = null;

        await starWarsClient.CreateBatch()
            .Query(c => c.Query.Film(filmID: new ID { Value = "1" })
                .Select(f => f.Title)
                .OnResult(r => title = r))
            .Query(c => c.Query.Person(personID: new ID { Value = "1" })
                .Select(p => p.Name)
                .OnResult(r => name = r))
            .Query(c => c.Query.AllPlanets(first: 3)
                .Select(e => e.Planets.Select(p => p.Name).ToList())
                .OnResult(r => planets = r))
            .ExecuteAsync();

        Console.WriteLine(title);
        Console.WriteLine(name);
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(", ", planets));
        """;

    private const string FirstBatchOutput = """
        A New Hope
        Luke Skywalker
        Tatooine, Alderaan, Yavin IV
        """;

    private const string FirstBatchQuery = """
        query($b0_filmID_1_1: ID $b1_personID_1_1: ID $b2_first_1_1: Int) {
          b0: film(filmID: $b0_filmID_1_1) { title }
          b1: person(personID: $b1_personID_1_1) { name }
          b2: allPlanets(first: $b2_first_1_1) { planets { name } }
        }
        """;

    private const string LoopCSharp = """
        var titles = new Dictionary<string, string>();
        var batch = starWarsClient.CreateBatch(o => o.MaxItemsPerRequest = 3);

        foreach (var id in new[] { "1", "2", "3", "4", "5", "6" })
        {
            batch.Query(c => c.Query.Film(filmID: new ID { Value = id })
                .Select(f => f.Title)
                .OnResult(r => titles[id] = r));
        }

        await batch.ExecuteAsync();

        foreach (var (id, title) in titles)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"{id}: {title}");
        }
        """;

    private const string LoopOutput = """
        1: A New Hope
        2: The Empire Strikes Back
        3: Return of the Jedi
        4: The Phantom Menace
        5: Attack of the Clones
        6: Revenge of the Sith
        """;

    private const string ConditionalCSharp = """
        var includeCrawl = false;
        string director = null;
        string crawl = null;

        await starWarsClient.CreateBatch()
            .Query(c => c.Query.Film(filmID: new ID { Value = "1" })
                .Select(f => f.Director)
                .OnResult(r => director = r))
            .QueryIf(c => c.Query.Film(filmID: new ID { Value = "1" })
                .Select(f => f.OpeningCrawl)
                .OnResult(r => crawl = r), includeCrawl)
            .ExecuteAsync();

        Console.WriteLine($"{director} / crawl loaded: {crawl != null}");
        """;

    private const string ConditionalOutput = """
        George Lucas / crawl loaded: False
        """;

    private const string ResultCSharp = """
        string found = null;
        string missing = "not called";

        var result = await starWarsClient.CreateBatch()
            .Query(c => c.Query.Film(filmID: new ID { Value = "1" })
                .Select(f => f.Title)
                .OnResult(r => found = r))
            .Query(c => c.Query.Film(filmID: new ID { Value = "99" })
                .Select(f => f.Title)
                .OnResult(r => missing = r))
            .ExecuteWithResultAsync();

        Console.WriteLine(found);
        Console.WriteLine(missing ?? "null");
        foreach (var error in result.Errors)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"{error.Message} (path: {string.Join("/", error.Path)})");
        }
        """;

    private const string ResultOutput = """
        A New Hope
        null
        No entry in local cache for https://www.swapi.tech/api/films/99 (path: b1)
        """;

    private const string OptionsCSharp = """
        var batch = starWarsClient.CreateBatch(options =>
        {
            options.Mode = GraphBatchMode.SingleRequest;
            options.MaxItemsPerRequest = 50;
            options.MaxDegreeOfParallelism = 2;
            options.SplitOnRejection = true;
        });
        """;
}
